<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gautami Balashri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gautami Balashri was a Satavahana queen, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a>. Her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> cave inscription, made in the reign of her grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasishthiputra-pulumavi">Vasishthiputra Pulumavi</a>, praises her son as a destroyer of the Shakas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavanas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pahlavas">Pahlavas</a> and an upholder of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>, and records her gift of a cave to Buddhist monks. It is a key source for the dynasty's history.</p></div>
